  <iati-identifier>NL-KVK-40409352-PRJ13-167-0002</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="NL-KVK-40409352" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Netherlands Red Cross</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ghana - Local urban WASH : connecting the most vulnerable to Water &amp; Sanitation utilities</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">3 communities in Accra and 3 communities in Kumasi</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ghana Water Company Limited - GWCL has been given the public mandate to provide well-regulated piped water services to all urban communities (&gt; 65 million people) in Ghana. In executing its mandate, GWCL strives towards an inclusive water service delivery in which special attention is paid to serving the Low-Income Urban Communities. The Low-Income Customer Support Department (LICSu)  of GWCL is tasked to take the lead in addressing the low coverage and improving the service in urban low-income communities. Vitens Evides International (VEI) one of the main Dutch water providers with international branches is a long-term partner of GWCL, having been actively involved in the development of the LICSu Department. Within this project VEI and GWCL partner with IFRC Ghana and NLRC to connect low income communities in Kumasi to water supply. Ghana RC will use access to commuities and CEA to engage the local communities so that they are aware of how to use the local water supply and ultimately stay connected.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kumasi , Accra</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Netherlands Red Cross (NLRC) recognizes the crucial role of public water and sanitation service providers in supplying sustainable water and sanitation services in urban contexts and specifically for the urban-poor. In addition, the NLRC sees the RCRC movement as a strong complimentary organization to public water and sanitation service providers, due to our volunteer base and  our expertise in community involvement which is  an expertise  often lacking at the more technocratic public service providers. NLRC can specifically support service providers  in the field of emergency preparedness and response, community engagement and accountability  behaviour change communication, and non-sewered sanitation/faecal sludge management. The concept note also underlines how supporting public water and sanitation service providers aligns beautifully with the new Water/WASH pillar in NLRCs strategy.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">With the urban population showing steady growth over the past decade, the Government of Ghana is faced with the challenge of coping with the growing demand for Water, Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) services, especially in deprived urban settlements. Currently, around 70% of the nation&apos;s urban population lives in congested, sub-standard structures with little incentive to invest in a more permanent solution. Most parts of the peri-urban areas fall outside the formal planning framework of the State authorities, and therefore lack legal standing in terms of utility services. As a result, the vast majority of the people without access to water and sanitation services are found in the poorer communities and delivering safe, sustainable and affordable water and sanitation services in poor urban communities remains a major challenge.</narrative>
  </description>
